     title: "The memory tool finds memory across short-term, Garura and Phoenix memory the way Claude Code searches"
     body: |
       ## What this delivers
-      The command-line tool from the contract ({item:2}), with a folder engine. It finds files, searches their text and reads exact lines — the way Claude Code does, with the same kind of tools (ADR 035 §4: "this is critical as this is why claude-code is so good"). It looks in short-term memory, then Garura memory, then Phoenix memory, and stops at the first tier that answers. It pulls a piece into Garura memory when it finds it in Phoenix memory, and it writes approved updates. Every result carries its tier, file, lines and fingerprint. It works the same from Claude Code and from Codex.
+      The command-line tool from the contract ({item:2}), with a folder engine. It finds files, searches their text and reads exact lines — the way Claude Code does, with the same kind of tools (ADR 035 §4: "this is critical as this is why claude-code is so good"). It looks in the current issue's short-term memory, then Garura memory, then Phoenix memory, and stops at the first tier that answers. Every lookup names its issue; short-term memory is that issue's folder only, never another issue's, while Garura and Phoenix memory are read in full (ADR 036 §1). It pulls a piece into Garura memory when it finds it in Phoenix memory, and it writes approved updates to their tier: the issue's short-term memory, or Garura or Phoenix memory for a promotion (ADR 036 §2–§3). Every result carries its tier, file, lines and fingerprint. It works the same from Claude Code and from Codex.
 
       ## Done when
       - Its tests pass on sample memory in all three tiers, including going up a tier and pulling a piece.
       - A missing tier gives an error, not an empty answer.
+      - A lookup never reads another issue's short-term memory. A glob or path that steps outside the issue (for example `../613/**`) is refused with `bad_request` (PR #666 review, TIC-7).
+      - A lookup that asks only Garura or Phoenix memory works without an issue, so work that starts before an issue exists can look things up (PR #666 review, TIC-3). This changes the contract; change it with its version.
       - A boundary check passes: nothing in its folder names Garura.
       - Pointing it at Griffin later means changing one setting.
 
@@ -70,17 +72,27 @@ stories:
     title: "When no memory answers, an agent raises a memory update a person approves, and then decides from memory"
     body: |
       ## What this delivers
-      ADR 035 §5. When no tier answers, an agent does not decide from the model or the web. It raises a **memory update**: what is missing, which tier it belongs in, a drafted entry, and where the draft came from (the person, the web, the model — marked). Nothing in it is used until a person approves it. The approved update is written to Garura memory (something only this project needs) or to Phoenix memory (something any project could use, as a commit in its repository), and the waiting work decides from memory. A person's answers to questions become Garura memory with stable ids, so they are never asked twice (the question store in `standards/rules/product.md`, rule 12). The knowledge-base proposal (`propose-kb-node`) becomes one kind of memory update. Until the memory review exists (the Memory view, feature plan item 11), the person approves by typing.
+      ADR 035 §5, as amended by ADR 036 §2–§4. An agent looks in the current issue's short-term memory, then Garura memory, then Phoenix memory. When no tier answers, it does not decide from the model or the web on its own. It researches — the web or the model may draft the answer — and raises a **memory update**: what is missing, a drafted entry, and where the draft came from (the person, the web, the model — marked). Nothing in it is used until a person approves it.
+
+      - The approved answer is written to the **issue's own short-term memory**, and the waiting work decides from memory (ADR 036 §2). A draft from the web or the model never goes straight to Garura or Phoenix memory.
+      - Moving an answer up — to Garura memory for something only this project needs, or to Phoenix memory for something any project could use (as a commit in its repository) — is a **promotion**. It is its own memory update, drafted from memory, approved by a person, and done outside the work (ADR 036 §3).
+      - A person's own answers to questions become Garura memory with stable ids, so they are never asked twice (ADR 036 §4; the question store in `standards/rules/product.md`, rule 12).
+      - The knowledge-base proposal (`propose-kb-node`) becomes one kind of memory update.
+      - Until the review tool's Memory and ADRs section exists ({item:11}), the person approves by typing.
+
+      The memory tool's contract ({item:2}, `core/tools/memory/contract/v1.json`) already holds these steps: `propose` → `decide` → `apply`, an update to short-term memory must name its issue, and a researched draft must target short-term memory.
 
       Settle here, from ADR 035's "Not decided here": whether the rest of a drive keeps going while one memory update waits for a person.
 
       ## Done when
-      - A gap, a memory update, an approval, a write and an answer from memory run end to end on a sample.
+      - A gap, a memory update, an approval, a write to the issue's short-term memory and an answer from memory run end to end on a sample. A later lookup in the same issue finds the answer; another issue does not.
+      - A promotion moves an approved answer from short-term memory to Garura memory as its own approved update.
+      - The approval record proves a person gave the word, not an agent. Today the contract's `decide` takes any name as `by` (PR #666 review, TIC-2): settle how a person's word is proven, and change the contract with its version if needed.
       - A rejected update writes nothing, and the work stays stopped at that point.
       - A sample run shows nothing taken from the model or the web without an approved update.
 
